n='nsec-wireless';kr=['https://www.krackattacks.com/'];dragon=['https://wpa3.mathyvanhoef.com/'];eap=rfc(5247)
edit(n,'why WPA2-Personal passwords can be cracked offline and WPA3\'s SAE cannot, and how 802.1X gives every user their own key.','how WPA2-Personal permits offline guessing, what correctly implemented SAE changes, and how enterprise authentication separates unicast keys.','ScopeSAEimplementationandgroupkeyexceptions.')
edit(n,'Wi-Fi has none. Every frame is radio, received by anyone in range, from the car park or the flat next door, often hundreds of metres away with a directional antenna.','Wi-Fi signals may extend beyond the intended premises. Capture depends on channel, signal, interference and receiver capabilities; being nearby does not guarantee reception of every frame.','Removeallframes/prevalence/rangedeterminism.')
edit(n,'**Confidentiality and integrity** of every frame over the air.','**Confidentiality and integrity** for protected data, with separate protection rules for management traffic; many headers and control frames remain exposed.','Noteveryframeprotected.')
edit(n,'by the birthday bound, there is a 50% chance of a repeat after only about 4,800 frames.','under independent uniform random IV selection with replacement, the birthday approximation gives a 50% collision chance after about 4,800 frames. Counter-based choices have different collision behavior until wraparound.','Birthdaymodelprecondition.')
edit(n,'Two frames with the same IV, XORed together, cancel the keystream.','For equal IVs under the same WEP key, XORing the encrypted payloads cancels their keystream and reveals the XOR of the plaintexts; recovering either plaintext needs further information.','XORnotdirectplaintext.')
edit(n,'**One static key for everyone**, typed into every device and never changed.','**Shared-key management** makes individual revocation and safe rotation difficult; WEP does not require that operators literally never change keys.','Neverchangednotprotocolrule.')
edit(n,'    return hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac(\n        "sha1", passphrase.encode(),\n        ssid.encode(), 4096, 32)','    password = passphrase.encode("ascii")\n    name = ssid.encode("ascii")\n    if not 8 <= len(password) <= 63:\n        raise ValueError("passphrase length")\n    if not 1 <= len(name) <= 32:\n        raise ValueError("SSID length")\n    return hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac(\n        "sha1", password, name, 4096, 32)','BoundtoyASCIIpassphraseSSIDdomain; avoidimplyarbitraryUTF8standard.')
edit(n,'This prints `f42c6fc52df0ebef`,','This ASCII-only passphrase example (not raw hexadecimal PSK provisioning) prints `f42c6fc52df0ebef`,','ScopekeyderivationAPI.')
edit(n,'so networks called `linksys` or `BTHub` share precomputed tables.','so a table for one exact SSID can be reused on other networks with that same SSID, not across arbitrary different names.','DifferentSSIDsdonotsharePMKtable.')
edit(n,'Both nonces are fresh, so every session gets a new PTK even though the PMK is fixed.','Fresh nonces separate PTK derivations for distinct handshakes under the same PMK; secure nonce generation and correct key-installation state are required.','Notautomaticallimplementations.')
edit(n,'The 4,096 PBKDF2 iterations slow each guess, but a high-end GPU still manages on the order of a million guesses a second. Since 2018 attackers often do not even need a client: the **PMKID** that many APs include in M1 is an HMAC of the PMK and the two MAC addresses, and can be requested directly from the AP.','The 4,096 PBKDF2 iterations add work per guess. The rate depends on hardware and implementation; the next example uses a hypothetical rate. In some configurations, a captured **PMKID**, derived using the PMK, a fixed label and both MAC addresses, supplies another offline verifier without a victim client handshake.','UnmeasuredGPUrate/prevalence;PMKIDhaslabeltruncatedHMAC.')
edit(n,'At 10^6 guesses a second,','At an assumed constant 10^6 distinct guesses a second,','Hypotheticalbenchmark.')
edit(n,'Real passwords are far weaker than random ones, so a dictionary attack usually finishes in minutes. Five random words, by contrast, are out of reach.','Human choices can be nonuniform and vulnerable to dictionary order. Uniformly choosing five words from a specified large list gives a much larger finite search space (calculated in the quiz); security still depends on the list, selection process and attacker resources.','Unsupportedusualminutesandabsoluteoutofreach.')
edit(n,"WPA2-Personal security therefore rests entirely on passphrase strength, and everyone who knows the passphrase can derive the PMK and, by watching a handshake, decrypt other users' traffic on the same network.","WPA2-Personal's resistance to this offline guessing attack depends on passphrase strength. An observer who knows the shared passphrase and captures the needed handshake can derive another client's link-layer keys; application TLS can still protect the application payload. Implementation and management-frame security also matter.",'Notentiresecurityonlypassword.')
edit(n,'implementations did so **every time M3 arrived**.','vulnerable implementations reinstalled a key on a retransmitted M3.','Notallimplementations.',kr)
edit(n,'Captured frames could then be decrypted; with TKIP or GCMP, frames could also be forged.','Nonce reuse can expose relationships between plaintexts and enable decryption with additional known or predictable data; the exact capability depends on cipher and traffic. KRACK also enabled forgery in affected TKIP/GCMP cases.','XORknownplaintextrequirement.',kr)
edit(n,'`wpa_supplicant` 2.4 and 2.5','affected `wpa_supplicant` versions 2.4 and later before fixes','Originalreported2.4andabove.',kr)
edit(n,'The fix was a software patch, mostly to clients: refuse to reinstall a key that is already in use.','Fixes ensure keys are not reinstalled in a way that resets their security state. Patch every affected device: client patches do not fix vulnerable AP-side Fast BSS Transition behavior, nor do ordinary AP patches automatically fix clients.','Allaffectedrolesneedpatch.',kr)
edit(n,'| Open Wi-Fi in clear | OWE (Enhanced Open) |','| Open Wi-Fi in clear | Separate Enhanced Open programme: OWE |','OWEnotWPA3Personalfeature.',['https://source.android.com/docs/core/connect/wifi-wpa3-owe'])
edit(n,'reveals nothing an eavesdropper can test guesses against.','is designed to resist passive offline dictionary verification when correctly implemented.','Dragonbloodimplementationexceptions.',dragon)
edit(n,'The resulting PMK is fresh per session, so learning the password later does not decrypt recorded traffic.','A fresh SAE exchange derives new key material and provides forward secrecy against later password disclosure under its security assumptions. Caching and roaming can reuse key material across associations.','Notfreshperassociation.',dragon)
edit(n,'**Protected Management Frames** (802.11w) authenticate deauthentication and disassociation frames.','**Protected Management Frames** (802.11w) protect robust management frames such as deauthentication/disassociation once the relevant security association is established. They do not prevent radio jamming or protect every pre-association/control frame.','PMFscope.')
edit(n,'The AP blocks everything except authentication traffic (EAPOL).','The controlled data port remains unauthorised while authentication proceeds; EAPOL and necessary link-management traffic are separate.','Noteveryframetypeblocked.',rfc(3748))
edit(n,'On success, RADIUS sends the AP a per-user key, which becomes that client\'s PMK.','With a key-generating EAP method and the illustrated RADIUS deployment, exported keying material reaches the authenticator over the AAA channel; the link layer derives/selects its PMK. RADIUS is a common backend, not a requirement of every 802.1X deployment.','MSKexport/PMKderivationbackendscope.',eap)
edit(n,"Every user and session gets a unique PMK, so one user cannot decrypt another's traffic.","Independent authentication credentials and pairwise key derivation prevent one ordinary user's credentials from deriving another's unicast keys. Group traffic can still use a shared GTK, and caching/roaming can reuse PMKs; isolation is not a claim that every frame has a unique per-user key.",'SharedGTKcounterexample/cachingscope.',eap)
edit(n,'MSCHAPv2 can be cracked offline (it reduces to breaking a single DES key). Push a profile that pins the CA and server name to every device, or use EAP-TLS.','Captured MSCHAPv2 challenge responses permit offline attacks on its DES-based construction, which can recover an NT password hash without necessarily recovering the plaintext password. Deploy a profile validating the intended CA and server name. EAP-TLS avoids password-based inner authentication but still requires server validation.','HashnotplaintextandEAPTLSnotverificationbypass.',rfc(2759))
edit(n,'so strength depends entirely on the passphrase.','so passphrase entropy limits resistance to that guessing attack; other security properties still depend on configuration and implementation.','Scopeentirelyclaim.')
edit(n,'it was fixed by client patches.','affected clients and APs require the relevant patches.','Clientpatchnotallcases.',kr)
edit(n,'## Key takeaways','> [!note] Content omitted after review\n> Universal GPU guessing rates, claims that dictionary attacks usually finish in minutes, and an absolute guarantee that a five-word passphrase is uncrackable are omitted. No hardware benchmark, password distribution or attacker budget was specified; the numerical exercise uses explicit hypothetical assumptions.\n\n## Key takeaways','Visiblemeasurementgap.')
